## Claim
Text: "Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump are banned from ever operating a charity again because they stole donations for children with cancer."
Claim author: Left Action
Claim date: October 20, 2020
Claim origin: https://archive.vn/ssfb8

## Initial Q&A
### Did a court or regulatory authority permanently ban Eric Trump from operating any charity?
Answer: No, a court did not permanently ban Eric Trump from operating any charity. According to the Snopes fact-check, the New York Supreme Court's final resolution in November 2019 did not bar Eric Trump from running charities in New York state; instead, he was required to undergo board training on fiduciary responsibilities.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-steal-kids-cancer-charity/

### Did a court or regulatory authority permanently ban Donald J. Trump Jr. from operating any charity?
Answer: No, a court or regulatory authority did not permanently ban Donald J. Trump Jr. from operating any charity. The final resolution of the New York Supreme Court case in November 2019 did not include such a ban; instead, it required him to undergo board training, and the request by the attorney general for a one-year ban was never granted. As Snopes notes, "the New York Supreme Court had not barred members of the Trump family from operating or running charities within the state."

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-steal-kids-cancer-charity/

### Did a court or regulatory authority permanently ban Ivanka Trump from operating any charity?
Answer: **No.** According to the Snopes fact-check, the final resolution of the case published by the New York Supreme Court on November 7, 2019, did **not** bar Ivanka Trump (or Eric Trump or President Trump) from operating any charity; instead, she was required to undergo “an in-person, interactive board training session pertaining to charitable organizations and the fiduciary responsibilities of those organizations’ directors and officers.”

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-steal-kids-cancer-charity/

### Was the alleged ban based on a finding that Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump stole donations intended for children with cancer?
Answer: No, the alleged ban was not based on a finding that Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump stole donations intended for children with cancer. According to FactCheck.org, the viral claim "inaccurately describes the outcome of a court case involving the Donald J. Trump Foundation, and conflates it with allegations about the Eric Trump Foundation." The settlement with New York's attorney general did not bar the Trump children from operating charities; it required mandatory training and imposed conditions on the president if he were to serve on a charity board. The allegations about "stealing from a kids cancer charity" stem from a separate Forbes investigation into the Eric Trump Foundation, which was not part of the lawsuit.

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2019/12/social-posts-distort-facts-on-trump-charities/

### What is the specific legal case or proceeding in which the ban was imposed on Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump?
Answer: The search result describes the lawsuit filed by the New York attorney general on June 14, 2018, which sought to ban Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump from serving as directors of a New York not-for-profit for one year (or until fiduciary training), but it does not indicate that a final legal proceeding or judgment had already imposed such a ban. Therefore, the information is insufficient to answer the Question.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2018/06/14/politics/new-york-lawsuit-trump-foundation/index.html

### Did any court or regulatory body issue a ruling that the Trump family members misappropriated funds designated for pediatric cancer charities?
Answer: No court or regulatory body has issued a ruling that the Trump family members misappropriated funds specifically designated for pediatric cancer charities. The lawsuit and settlement involving the Donald J. Trump Foundation concerned improper political and self-dealing activities, not a children’s cancer charity, and while Eric Trump’s foundation faced scrutiny over its handling of funds for St. Jude, no ruling on misappropriation of such funds was made.

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/oct/24/facebook-posts/trump-family-disallowed-operating-charities-new-yo/

### Is the ban truly permanent, or does it have a fixed duration or conditions under which it could be lifted?
Answer: The ban is not permanent. According to the Politifact article, the lawsuit sought to temporarily prohibit Donald Trump from serving in New York charities for ten years and his three eldest children for one year, with the children’s ban subject to suspension if they completed training on fiduciary duties. The settlement later imposed restrictions and mandatory training, but no permanent ban was enacted.

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/oct/24/facebook-posts/trump-family-disallowed-operating-charities-new-yo/

### Were Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump personally named in a lawsuit or settlement concerning the Trump Foundation’s alleged misuse of cancer-related donations?
Answer: Yes, Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump were personally named as defendants in the New York Attorney General’s 2018 lawsuit against the Trump Foundation, which alleged that they violated state and federal charities law, including the misuse of charitable assets; the suit specifically sought to ban them from serving as directors of a New York not-for-profit for one year or until they received fiduciary training.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2018/06/14/politics/new-york-lawsuit-trump-foundation/index.html

### Did the New York Attorney General’s Office or any other authority obtain a court order banning the three individuals from serving as directors or officers of a charity?
Answer: Based on the Snopes fact-check article, the court did **not** ban Eric Trump, Donald Trump Jr., or Ivanka Trump from serving as directors or officers of a charity. The article states that the New York Attorney General's request to "enjoin its board members from future service as a director of any not-for-profit" was not included in the final resolution, and that "the state's Supreme Court published a final resolution of the case, one which did not bar either President Trump or his children Eric and Ivanka, from running charities in the state of New York."

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-steal-kids-cancer-charity/

### Does any official document (e.g., court judgment, consent decree, or attorney general press release) state that Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump stole donations meant for children with cancer?
Answer: No official document—such as a court judgment, consent decree, or attorney general press release—states that Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., or Ivanka Trump stole donations meant for children with cancer. The New York Attorney General’s lawsuit against the Trump Foundation alleged “self-dealing” and improper practices, not theft, and the final court resolution did not include any finding of stealing or ban from operating charities. As the Snopes article notes, “nobody involved is accused of ‘stealing,’ as such.”

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-steal-kids-cancer-charity/

## Final Judgement
The fact-check evidence consistently shows that no court or regulatory authority permanently banned Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., or Ivanka Trump from operating a charity. The New York Supreme Court's final resolution in November 2019 did not impose such a ban; instead, it required board training on fiduciary responsibilities. The New York Attorney General's request for a one-year ban was never granted. Furthermore, no official document or ruling found that they stole donations intended for children with cancer; the lawsuit concerned improper political and self-dealing activities by the Donald J. Trump Foundation, not a children's cancer charity, and separate allegations about the Eric Trump Foundation were not part of the case. The claim is therefore false.

